==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|hidous|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|xno|en}} {{term|hidous||lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|hideus|lang=fro}}, {{term|hydus||that which inspires terror|lang=fro}}, from earlier {{term|hisdos|lang=fro}}, from {{term|hisda||lang=fro|horror, fear}}, of uncertain and disputed origin. Probably of {{etyl|gem}} origin, from {{etyl|frk}} *{{term||egisda, *egisida|terror, fright|lang=frk}}, from {{proto|Germanic|agisiþō|horror, terror|lang=en}} from {{proto|Germanic|agisōjanan|to frighten, terrorise|lang=en}} from {{proto|Germanic|title=Proto-Germanic base|agiz-|terror, fear|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|ag(')h-|to frighten|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{goh}} {{term|egisa|||lang=goh}}, {{term|egidi|egidī|horror|lang=goh}}, {{ang}} {{term|egesa||fear, dread|lang=ang}}, {{got}} {{term|𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃||tr=agis|fear, terror|lang=got|sc=Goth}}.

Alternative etymology cites possbile derivation from {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|hispidosus||rugged|lang=la}}, from {{term|hispidus||lang=la|rough, bristly}}, yet the semantic evolution is more difficult to trace. <!--fro?: ''[[hidous]], [[hidos]], [[hidus]], [[hisdos]], [[hisdous]]'', {{etyl|fr}} ''[[hideux]]'': confer {{etyl|fro}} ''[[hide]]'', {{term|hisde||fright}}; , {{etyl|en|-}}-->

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈhɪd.iː.ʌs/}} {{SAMPA|/"hId.i:.Vs/}}
* {{audio|en-us-hideous.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Frightful; shocking; extremely [[ugly]].
#: A piteous and hideous spectacle." [[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay]].
# Distressing or offensive to the ear; horrible.
#* '''1719''', [[w:Daniel Defoe|Daniel Defoe]], ''[[w:Robinson Crusoe|Robinson Crusoe]]''
#*: ''He started up, growling at first, but finding his leg broken, fell down again; and then got upon three legs, and gave the most '''hideous''' roar that ever I heard.''
# Hateful; shocking.
#: ''Sure, you have some hideous matter to deliver.'' - [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]]

